Transport Exposure stdio None. The client is the parent process; nothing else can connect. HTTP Bound to <code>127.0.0.1</code>: remote machines cannot reach it, but every local process can. <p>The HTTP port is therefore an unauthenticated file-system capability for any script, any other user of a shared workstation, and any web page able to issue a request to localhost. Require a token:</p> <pre><code># Standalone server: print a freshly generated token
fmutool-mcp --transport http --token generate

# GUI assistant: same thing, through the environment
FMUCONTAINER_MCP_TOKEN=generate fmucontainer-gui
</code></pre> <p>The token is written to the log (the log window of the Container Builder, or stderr for the standalone server); configure your client to send it:</p> <pre><code>{
  "servers": {
    "fmutool": {
      "type": "http",
      "url": "http://127.0.0.1:8765/mcp",
      "headers": { "Authorization": "Bearer &lt;the-token&gt;" }
    }
  }
}
</code></pre> <p>A token of your own works too (<code>--token my-long-shared-secret</code>), as long as it is at least 16 characters: a short one only provides the illusion of protection, so it is rejected.</p> <p>Without a token the server still starts — enabling the assistant must not require ceremony — but it says clearly in the log that the port is open.</p>
